From 5917edd6e5ed156ca22c3ddfe53dda50ef56f0d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 18:45:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fma: the absorbability fixpoint is a reader closure, not
 a poison walk

The multi-use rule shipped with badness propagating from a Neg's SOURCE
to its reader -- the exact inverse of the invariant the same change
documented. A Neg read by an fma argument slot AND by a Neg that
survives the sweep stayed 'absorbable', was rewritten at the site and
deleted, and the surviving link kept naming it: valid C of the shape
  double t = -x, u = -t;
  double r = __builtin_fma(t, b, c);
  return r + u;
ICEs at -O1 and above (x86 codegen: value has no register, stack slot,
Copy, or GlobalAddr definition). The front-end emits bare Negs with no
neg-of-neg fold and the peel is the last IR transform, so nothing
between lowering and codegen repairs or reports it.

Flipping the poison edge is necessary but not sufficient: a surviving
reader that no fma chain reaches is never poisoned and still orphans
its source. The rule that is sound in every direction is the GREATEST
FIXPOINT over the reader closure and the site reachability together:
a Neg may be deleted iff (i) none of its reads is non-peeling, (ii)
every reader of it is deleted with it, and (iii) some fma-argument
slot reaches it through deleted Negs. (ii) and (iii) are mutually
recursive -- dropping a Neg pins its source and shortens every chain
that descended through it -- so iterate removals to stability. The
computation is O(N+E) per round over a precomputed reader adjacency;
at most one productive round per Neg. tools/ir_shape_check.py
enumerates the chain shape space and keeps both failure modes (the
shipped direction, the one-line repair) pinned as negative controls:
738/4545 and 90/4545 unsafe there, 0 for the closure -- which also
folds MORE than the shipped rule (1752 vs 1722 shapes), because the
source-poisoned direction over-pinned chains whose inner link alone
was external. The pinned-inner shape now compiles to one sign mask
and vfnmadd reading the materialised inner, the reference compiler's
exact form.

The peel now also absorbs Negs off already-Signed FmaScalar sites: the
families compose by XOR (Signed(np, na) absorbing one more Neg on a
product slot is Signed(np^1, na)) and a full cancel normalises to the
plain spelling. This makes the FmaArg classification TRUE -- every
read classified as a peelable argument slot really does disappear with
the rewrite -- instead of coincidentally conservative: the vectorizer's
scalar remainder mirror emits Signed sites whose args can be Neg
values, sound today only because the vector main lanes pin the Neg
(VecMadd reads classify as Other), and the planned map-side multi-use
peel removes exactly that pin.

The sweep's precondition is now self-enforcing: an unconditional
assert re-derives, from the ACTUAL decision set, that no absorbed Neg
has a non-peeling read and that every reader of one is absorbed with
it (unconditional rather than debug_assert because the fastbuild/test
profile compiles debug-assertions out, and this is a release-miscompile
class that should fail loud, per the house discipline). Both peel call
sites (-O1 tier and the -O2+ Phase 11f tail) now run verify_after_pass:
the peel is the last IR transform and nothing downstream re-checks the
module, so a malformed rewrite here was invisible to CCC_VERIFY_IR.

Unit tests 16 -> 21: the shipped defect's own shape (the site must
read the INNER link for the shape to discriminate), the dead-outer-
reader control, the pinned-inner fold, and the two already-Signed XOR
compositions; all 16 prior expectations unchanged.
